   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the election law, in relation to requiring the board of\nelections to mail notice of ensuing primary and general elections and\nregistration information to active voters between the third Tuesday in April\nand the second Friday in May each year",
    "basePrintNo": "S8552",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8552-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §4-117, El L",
    "lawSection": "Election Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8552\n\nSPONSOR: MYRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the election law, in relation to requiring the board of\nelections to mail notice of ensuing primary and general elections and\nregistration information to active voters between the third Tuesday in\nApril and the second Friday in May each year\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo require the board of elections to mail notice of ensuing primaries\nand general elections as well as registration information to active\nvoters between the third Tuesday in April and the second Friday in May\neach year\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends Subdivision. 1 of section 4-117 of the election law, as\namended by chapter 200 of the laws of 2020, to change the date range the\nboard of elections must send notice of elections and.  registration\ninformation from \"not less than sixty-five days nor more than seventy\ndays before the primary election\" to \"between the third Tuesday i- April\nand the second Friday in Ma- \"\n\nSection 2 provides for the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nEvery year, each board of elections sends an annual informational mail-\ning to voters informing them of important dates and voting hours in the\nelection calendar. The card also tells voters where to vote.  If the\ncard does not list early voting sites, it must inform voters where to\nobtain early voting information. Under current law, boards must mail\nthis card not less than 65 nor more than 70 days before the primary,\nwhich in 2022 is between April 19 and April 24. Early voting sites must\nbe designated by May I (see 8-600).\n\nMoving the deadline into May allows boards to send the cards after the\nearly voting site designation deadline. This year in particular, there\nis a disruption of board workflows due to redistricting, which causes a\nlarger than normal number of poll site changes. This, coupled with\nsupply-chain. caused increases in time needed for vendors to perform the\nprinting and mailing services needed to send the cards, makes an exten-\nsion of the mail check period a very practical help for boards in their\neffort to pro de voters accurate information.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
